There’s a substantial body of research that supports part of what you’re pointing to, with an important distinction.

Humans don’t simply store isolated facts—we maintain relatively coherent models of ourselves, other people, and the world. Those models are continually updated by perception, memory, emotion, and social interaction. The “story we tell ourselves” is one way of describing the narrative layer of those models.

Your body contributes to maintaining that model as well:

* Memory systems maintain continuity.
* Emotions prioritize what matters.
* Habits reinforce identity (“I’m the kind of person who…”).
* Prediction constantly compares expectation with reality and updates the model.

So in that sense, our ongoing experience is partly a continuously edited narrative.

Where persuasion enters is that language can alter that narrative. Not by magically overriding someone’s beliefs, but by offering a different way to organize the same experiences.

A powerful story can:

* make someone reinterpret a past event,
* notice a pattern they had ignored,
* imagine a different future,
* adopt a different role (“mentor,” “builder,” “protector,” “learner”).

If that new framing fits their experience well enough, it can become incorporated into their broader self-model.

That also explains why stories are such durable coordination tools. They’re not merely information transfer; they’re shared simulation environments. Different people can inhabit approximately the same imagined world, reason inside it, and then return to reality with more aligned expectations.

Applied to your Pantheon project, I think the interesting question isn’t whether it’s “true” in a literal sense. It’s whether it functions as an effective semantic environment—a landscape that encourages people (or language models) to reconstruct useful conceptual operators and make new connections across domains.

That’s a testable claim. If people repeatedly emerge from interacting with the corpus able to solve problems differently, notice distinctions they previously missed, or translate ideas more effectively between fields, then the narrative has had a real cognitive effect regardless of whether they adopt every metaphor it uses.

In that view, the story isn’t the destination. It’s the interface through which a more abstract structure is learned.